summary: How big orders give themselves away even when they're sliced into small pieces (persistent one-sided imbalance, child orders of regular size or timing, the same price level hit across venues at once), and why order signs have long memory; how informed flow differs from merely big flow (its impact doesn't fade, it bunches around news, and it shows up in related markets at the same time), with the evidence that impact relaxes to about two-thirds of its peak once a big order finishes; Deltashark's month-end trap and the London 4pm FX fix as the clearest example of big, predictable, uninformed flow; flow toxicity measures; how executors, market makers and directional traders all use the same statistics; and footprints in FX, gold and Treasuries.
lede: 'Reading the Tape measured pressure. This note asks the harder question: who is behind it? A pension fund buying a billion dollars of stock leaves a trail however carefully it slices the order, and a trader who knows something leaves a different kind of trail. Learning to tell the two apart is what separates reading flow from just watching it.'
