quiz:
  - q: 'A fund slices a large buy order into small pieces of random sizes at random times. Which footprint is it still most likely to leave?'
    opts: ['Lots of trades of exactly the same size', 'A run of net buying that lasts far longer than chance would allow, because the total still has to be bought', 'Nothing at all: randomising hides it completely']
    right: 1
    why: 'Randomising size and timing hides the obvious patterns, but not the total. If the fund must buy 30,000 shares in half an hour, there will be persistent one-sided imbalance across many intervals. That persistence is also why order signs have long memory: one big order split into many small ones keeps pushing in the same direction.'
  - q: 'Two orders push a stock up by the same amount while they''re being executed. Afterwards, one''s impact stays and the other''s mostly fades. Which is more likely to have been informed?'
    opts: ['The one whose impact stayed: informed trading moves the price to a new fair value, so it doesn''t snap back', 'The one whose impact faded', 'You can''t ever tell']
    right: 0
    why: 'Deltashark''s key distinction: informed flow''s impact doesn''t revert, because the price has learned something. Uninformed flow (a rebalance, a cash flow) pushes the price temporarily and the market pulls it back once the pressure stops. On average across all big orders, impact settles at about two-thirds of its peak.'
  - q: 'Ten minutes before the London 4pm fix on the last day of the month, EUR/USD drops sharply with no news. What''s the most sensible reading?'
    opts: ['Someone knows bad news about the euro', 'The euro is starting a new downtrend', 'Probably predictable month-end hedging flow: big, one-sided and uninformed, so the move is likely to be temporary']
    right: 2
    why: 'Month-end fixes concentrate huge benchmark and hedging orders into a few minutes. Research (Melvin and Prins, Evans) found large, predictable moves before month-end fixes that aren''t driven by news. A sharp move into the fix with no catalyst is usually price pressure, not information, which is exactly Deltashark''s month-end trap.'

