{% sec 1, "Every Big Order Leaves Tracks", "Why can't a large order hide, even when it's cut into small pieces?", "Every big order leaves tracks" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Imagine moving house without a removal van. You make thirty car trips over a weekend, each with a few boxes, trying not to make a fuss. No single trip looks unusual. But the neighbours notice the same car leaving every forty minutes, always loaded, always going the same way. You split the job up, and the splitting is what gives you away.</p>
    <p>Big orders in markets work the same way. A fund that wants to buy a million shares doesn't send one order; it sends a {% gl "metaorder" %}metaorder{% endgl %} (the whole job) as hundreds of small {% gl "child order" %}child orders{% endgl %}, as Algorithmic Execution explained. Deltashark lists the footprints that leak even so:</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>Persistent same-sign imbalance<span class="why">Net buying interval after interval, longer than chance would produce. The total still has to be bought, however it's sliced.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>Child orders of suspiciously regular size or timing<span class="why">500 shares every 30 seconds is a pattern any program can find.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>The same side taken at several venues within milliseconds<span class="why">A router sweeping every exchange's offer at once shows a buyer who wants more than one venue can give.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The first footprint has been measured across whole markets. Buy and sell orders don't arrive like coin flips: if the last market order was a buy, the next one is more likely to be a buy too, and this effect fades only very slowly, over thousands of trades. Researchers call this the {% gl "long memory" %}long memory{% endgl %} of order flow. Fabrizio Lillo, Szabolcs Mike and Doyne Farmer (2005) showed it follows naturally from order splitting: when big orders are broken into pieces and the sizes of big orders vary enormously, the signs of trades end up correlated over very long stretches.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

